    },
    "description": "The Payment Gateway receives an authorization request containing a customer identifier, amount, and currency. The Request Parser converts each field, the Account Policy checks whether the account is open, and the Ledger Reserve service enforces available balance and daily limit rules. Any of these stages may reject the request, and the gateway must return a machine-readable failure category, affected field, and diagnostic detail to the API adapter. The API adapter maps unknown-category failures to HTTP 500, but maps known business failures to 400, 409, or 422 responses. It must never infer a category by matching an error message, because downstream clients branch on the category to decide whether to correct the request, refresh an account, or contact support. A request that fails validation must not reach Ledger Reserve, and multiple field errors reported together must retain separate field identities. Design the boundary between Payment Gateway, Account Policy, Ledger Reserve, and API Adapter so every expected rejection reaches the adapter intact while genuine programming faults remain distinguishable from recoverable business failures.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose how each expected payment rejection is represented and propagated so its category and field survive the full parsing, validation, and reservation flow.",
    "notes": "Either fits because each expected rejection carries a machine-readable category and field through parsing, policy, and reservation for downstream branching; result is the near-identical sibling and guard-clause covers the early-rejection axis."
  },
